service level objectives or SLO s are  one of the fundamental principles of  site reliability engineering will use  them to precisely quantify the  reliability target we want to achieve in  our service but how do we measure them  and what do we do when our SLO  monitoring tells us that there's a  problem with service monitoring we can  track our SLO s and air budgets and  quickly respond to our service is having  an issue this is a stack dr first let's  review the basics you may have heard of  SL o--'s but what's an SLI let's clearly  define both of those our overall  objective here is to accurately quantify  reliability and measure our user  happiness the actual thing we'll use as  a proxy for that it's called the service  level indicator or SLI we need to define  that indicator for our service an easy  place to start is by using one of our  golden signals we'll put the link to the  monitoring chapter of the SR e book  where those are explained in a notes  below you can also watch the excellent  video created by Liz and Seth that  explains this topic in greater detail  we'll link to that too  generally SL eyes fall into two  categories request based SL is measure  the fraction of good units of work  against the total for example what  percentage of a request in a HTTP  endpoint were successful in a given time  window window based SL eyes measure the  fraction of time intervals during which  the service meets some threshold over a  given time window let's use an example  if we have an app engine service we can  use the response count metric as our SLI  specifically we can count our good  responses by filtering that metric for  only responses that return an HTTP 200  and use the unfiltered value to count  our total responses we divide the two to  arrive at the fraction or ratio of good  to total and that's our SLI now let's  talk about SL owes a service level  objective is the target value for SLI  measured over time it's generally  comprised of three things the SLI 